\par\par \b \uc2 India  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  pushes rupee to 3-wk closing low  \b0\par\par\uc2 357 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2006\par 13:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Dec 11 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee fell to a 3-week closing low on Monday as dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had continued to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market even after its surprise move last week to tighten monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 A 2.9 percent drop in the stock market did not help sentiment either, although traders expect steady foreign flows due to an equity offering by Cairns India and Oracle Corp's bid to lift its stake in Indian banking software provider i-flex solutions Ltd to provide support in the next few weeks.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee <INR=IN> ended around 0.2 percent lower at 44.8350/8450 per dollar, its lowest close since Nov. 21 when it finished at 44.86/87.  \par \par \uc2 But it is still nearly 5 percent above a three-year low of 47.04 hit on July 19, with its gains helped by foreign inflows, the bulk of which have been mopped up by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "One thought that after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to tighten policy to fight inflation, the authorities would ease their grip on the rupee," said a chief dealer at a company.  \par \par \uc2 "But no such thing happened. They in fact drove the rupee down to around 44.85 from 44.78 in thin trade towards close."  \par \par \uc2 When the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buys dollars, normally through state-run banks, it pumps rupees into the banking system. This can add to inflation pressures and puts upward pressure on money supply growth.  \par \par \uc2 After markets had shut on Friday, the RBI announced a two-stage increase in the cash reserve ratio, that sets how much banks must keep with it, by 50 basis points to 5.50 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "The RBI's reluctance to let the rupee appreciate substantially in the light of strong capital inflows will only make conducting monetary policy more challenging for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Rajeev Malik, senior economist at JP Morgan, Singapore wrote in a note.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE|LANGEN|ABN|M|RBN|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061214e2cb002z0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
