\par\par \b \uc2 Cbank hand pushes Indian rupee to 1-wk closing low  \b0\par\par\uc2 344 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Dezember 2006\par 04:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Dec 6 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee <INR=IN> finished at a one-week closing low against the dollar on Wednesday after suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted a wave of short covering, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee ended at 44.70/71 per dollar, 0.4 percent weaker than Tuesday's finish of 44.52/53, which was an 8-\u189\'00\'BD month closing high.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  It touched an intra-day peak of 44.4850 -- its highest\par level since Nov. 10 -- before sustained \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  drove it\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 down, dealers said. Some traders said dollar demand from oil firms also weighed on the rupee.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market went short (on the dollar) after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  let the rupee close at a multi-month high yesterday," said a chief dealer at a large corporate house.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But once they (the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) started buying dollars at 44.54, driving it down to 44.70, banks and corporates rushed in to cover their positions. It was a cat-and-mouse game and the market was caught on the wrong side and the shorts got squeezed."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which usually \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through state-run banks, and was also suspected of heavy rupee selling in November. Analysts said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was a factor in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves jumping by over $5.5 billion from late October to a record $172.8 billion on Nov. 24.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders are wary that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could once again intensify its dollar-buying, especially since large foreign inflows are expected soon for an initial public offering by the Indian subsidiary of Cairns Energy Plc.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign funds have invested more than $8 billion in local shares this year, including $2 billion in November.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's main stock index <.BSESN> rose to a record high on Wednesday, and has risen more than 48 percent in 2006.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ------------------------------------------------------------\par                      MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (vs $)             <INR=IN>\par  ------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|RBN|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061209e2c60002b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
