\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee up on weak dlr but oil limits gains  \b0\par\par\uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2006\par 04:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to open)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Dec 4 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee rose on Monday after the dollar fell against the euro on expectations of a cut in U.S. interest rates, but traders said the rupee's gains were checked by higher global oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to a fresh 20-month low against the euro, after soft U.S. factory data reinforced expectations the Federal Reserve may cut interest rates next year.  \par \par \uc2 At 10 a.m. (0430 GMT), the partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> was at 44.63/64 per dollar, firmer than Friday's close of 44.655/665.  \par \par \uc2 "The rise in oil prices has curbed the rupee's gains today. Had oil been lower, we may have seen the rupee strengthening above 44.60 in opening trade," the chief dealer at a private bank said.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. crude <CLc1> traded above $63 a barrel on Monday. Traders expect oil refiners firms to buy dollars during the day, which limited dollar sales by banks. Oil is India's biggest import and higher prices raise the risk of widening the trade deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Sentiment was also wary about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check the rupee's rise. Strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  forced the rupee down from a near eight-month high of 44.35 in November.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through a clutch of state-owned banks. It buys or sells dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to maintain India's export competitiveness in global trade.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said they would eye local share prices to gauge its impact on capital inflows. Foreign investors have bought a net $8.7 billion of local stocks during 2006, helping the rupee recover from a three-year low of 47.04, hit in July.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign fund inflows have been encouraged by the main stocks index <.BSESN> hitting record highs. It hit a new record high of 13,911.64 points in early trade on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ---------------------------------------------------------------\par  -                      MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (vs $)             <INR=IN>\par  ---------------------------------------------------------------\par  -- ((Reporting by Anurag Joshi, editing by Ranjit Gangadharan;\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reuters Messaging: anurag.joshi.reuters.com@reuters.net; Tel: +91-22 6636-9038))  \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061207e2c40005z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
