\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee at 2-\u189\'00\'BD week lows on cbank, importers  \b0\par\par\uc2 345 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2006\par 04:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Nov 14 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee extended losses into a third straight session on Tuesday, falling to its lowest in more than two weeks, as sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  forced large corporates to buy dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars and sold rupees for a third consecutive session, taking the rupee further away from recent eight-month peaks and prompting more importers to rush to buy dollars to cover positions.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee <INR=IN> ended at 45.18/20 against the dollar, down 0.5 percent from the previous close. In morning trade it hit 45.23, its lowest since Oct. 27 according to Reuters data. At Tuesday's low it has shaved off 1.9 percent from a peak of 44.35 hit on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "There were some large companies buying dollars for most of the day," said a chief trader at an Indian private sector bank.  \par \par \uc2 "Towards close the state-run banks, which usually act on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bought dollars at 45.17/18 levels. We expect the rupee to weaken to 45.35/40 levels in the next few days."  \par \par \uc2 If it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually does so through state-run banks. The Reserve Bank of India does not have an explicit target or range for the rupee, but says it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth volatility and prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from straying too far from what it considers fair value.  \par \par \uc2 According to analysts, the rupee is overvalued by nearly 8 percent on a trade-weighted basis and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be keen to check any gain.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee had been rallying strongly last week on weighty foreign inflows into the stock market <.BSESN>, which is trading at record highs. Net foreign buying of local equities has topped $7 billion so far in 2006.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ------------------------------------------------------------\par                  MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (vs $)             <INR=IN>\par  ------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|Table|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061117e2be0007r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
