\par\par \b \uc2 Shares send Indian rupee to nearly 5-month high  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Saikat Chatterjee  \par \uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2006\par 05:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with fresh quotes, closing prices)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Oct 13 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee rose to its strongest in nearly five months on Friday, underpinned by cheaper oil and propelled by foreign fund inflows which sent stocks soaring to record highs.  \par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> ended at 45.41/42 a dollar after touching its highest since May 19 at 45.40, 0.4 percent firmer than its previous close of 45.61/62.  \par \par \uc2 "The mood is very, very, very bullish as foreign fund inflows are robust. We may see the rupee establish a (higher) trading band of 45.25-45.55 in the near term," said V. Rajagopal, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Kotak Mahindra Bank.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee has gained 3.6 percent from a three-year low of 47.04 set in July, with the pace escalating in the past two months on a pick-up in foreign fund inflows.  \par \par \uc2 But some say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step in to slow the rise and protect export competitiveness.  \par \par \uc2 "The rupee's gains have been unmatched by other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that may spur the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to moderate the sharp rise," said Kotak's Rajagopal.  \par \par \uc2 According to a JP Morgan six-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  real effective exchange rate <JPMREER>, the rupee is overvalued at nearly 7 percent, more than the 5 percent seen a month ago.  \par \par \uc2 CAPITAL INJECTION  \par \par \uc2 Recovering from a savage correction in May, the stock market ended at a record on Friday, driven by strong corporate earnings which have boosted hopes foreign funds will increase their investment in India.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark 30-share index <.BSESN> has risen about 43 percent from a June low, with foreign funds pumping more than $2.4 billion into Indian equities since August, compared with net investments of $2.8 billion in the first seven months of 2006.  \par \par \uc2 Big capital inflows have helped fund the widening current account deficit, which for the fiscal year 2005/06 was the highest since the country opened its economy in 1991. It doubled to $10.61 billion from $5.4 billion a year earlier.  \par \par \uc2 "The capital inflows have been responsible for the rupee rally. It is also benefiting from retreating oil prices, a positive for both fiscal finances and its balance of payments," DBS said in a recent note.  \par \par \uc2 DBS expects the rupee to rise to 45 per dollar by the end of 2006.  \par \par \uc2 CHEAPER OIL TO FUEL RISE?  \par \par \uc2 Lower oil prices help in reducing India's oil import bill and relieve pressure on the current account deficit. The country imports more than two-thirds of its oil.  \par \par \uc2 "With oil prices having fallen nearly 25 percent over the past eight weeks, the resulting shift in the demand/supply balance in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is significantly bullish," said JP Morgan in a recent note. It estimates dollar demand from oil importers alone contributes nearly $1 billion per week to overall dollar demand.  \par \par \uc2 Oil <CLc1> was trading around $58 a barrel, more than 25 percent down from a July peak of $78.40 per barrel.  \par \par \uc2 Still, some believe the rupee rally may be on its last legs with oil stabilising and as local stocks become expensive.  \par \par \uc2 "With the year-end nearing, fund managers may take some money off the table, and with oil prices likely stabilising around these levels, the rupee may be nearing the end of the rally," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro Singapore.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ---------------------------------------------------------------\par  --                     MARKET SNAPSHOT    Bombay Sensitive\par Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (vs $)             <INR=IN>\par ----------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - ((Editing by Charlotte Cooper; Reuters Messaging: saikat.chatterjee@reuters.com; Tel: +91-22 6636-9035))  \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|SNS|SNI|ABX|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061016e2ad0004w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
