\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee ends lower on dollar gains, arbitrage  \b0\par\par\uc2 372 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 2006\par 05:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2   (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI, July 24 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee fell on Monday on the dollar's rebound against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from oil companies, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said a stronger dollar in the offshore non-deliverable forwards <PNDF> market also weakened the rupee as it provided traders an arbitrage opportunity to buy dollars onshore and sell them overseas.  \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a fall in shares in early trade triggered the rupee's slide and heavy dollar demand throughout the day kept the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak.  \par \par \uc2  The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> ended at 46.91/92 per dollar, retreating from the day's low of 46.98, but still weaker than Friday's close of 46.77/78. It hit a three-year low of 47.04 last week.  \par \par \uc2  "The undertone for the rupee is still bearish. I don't think the interest rate factor will move the rupee much as the rupee is a largely flow-driven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a dealer with a private sector bank said, referring to Tuesday's quarterly monetary policy review.  \par \par \uc2  Dealers said exporters and some state-run banks sold dollars when the rupee weakened past 46.95, but it was not clear whether the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sold on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks to curb excess volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll last week showed 12 out 15 analysts forecast a 25 basis points increase in the reverse repo rate to 6.0 percent. But some in the market now expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might leave rates unchanged. It last raised the reverse repo rate by 25 basis points on June 8.  \par \par \uc2  The main share index <.BSESN> ended 1.3 percent down on Monday. The rupee's fortunes this year have largely mirrored share-related flows.  \par \par \uc2  Oil was just under $74 a barrel on Monday. India imports about 70 percent of its oil and high prices could depress the rupee.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  --------------------------------------------------------------\par                  MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  ------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060727e27o00060\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
