\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee fall seen adding to case for rate rise  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Anurag Joshi  \par \uc2 691 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 2006\par 13:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, June 1 (Reuters) - A rapid fall in India's rupee to a three-year low against the dollar <INR=IN> this week adds to pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee's drop will raise import prices in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  terms, fuelling inflation that is already being fired up by strong economic growth and a renewed surge in world oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 After an emerging markets sell-off that sucked in the rupee, analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to check any further sharp losses.  \par \par \uc2 But selling dollars and soaking up rupees will put upward pressure on short-term market rates by reducing the supply of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "If the kind of moves we have seen over the last couple of weeks build into a sustained negative environment, with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling dollars, then the monetary policy will have to be suitably adjusted," said Sanjeet Singh, head of research at ICICI Securities.  \par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> fell more than three percent in May to touch 46.57 on Wednesday, its lowest since June 2003, as foreign funds pulled money out of the stock market.  \par \par \uc2 The three-year low came on the same day data showed the economy grew in January-March at a higher-than-expected annual rate of 9.3 percent, which analysts said cemented expectations for a quarter-point rise in rates when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reviews policy on July 25.  \par \par \uc2 India's benchmark short-term rate stands at 5.5 percent and was last raised in January. Since then the rupee has fallen around 5 percent against the dollar and some analysts see it dropping to 47.50 by December if foreign funds continue to see emerging markets as too risky.  \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of India left rates unchanged in April but said India could not be out of step with global developments like U.S. interest rates and crude oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 RISING TRADE DEFICIT, INFLATION  \par \par \uc2 India's annual inflation rate, based on wholesale prices and reported weekly, rose to 4.32 percent on May 13, its highest level since February.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say if an expected rise in domestic fuel prices is factored in, the inflation rate could rise to 5 percent by the end of September.  \par \par \uc2 Economists say rising imports reflect the fast pace of the economy's growth but a weaker rupee makes oil imports costlier.  \par \par \uc2 Oil is India's biggest import and analysts expect the rising oil bill and strong import demand to widen the trade gap to $59.1 billion in the year ending March 2007 from $55 billion last year.  \par \par \uc2 With inflationary pressures building, the Reserve Bank of India is expected to continue raising rates after July, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "The RBI is on a tightening trajectory and we forecast a further 25-50 basis points in rate hikes during FY07," said Ashish Agrawal, Merrill Lynch's fixed income analyst in Mumbai.  \par \par \uc2 Robust foreign fund inflows into the stock market had been cushioning the rupee from the impact of a widening current account deficit, caused mainly by high oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 Now that funds are leaving for less risky markets, the current account is under pressure, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "The core problem is that (India's) current account deficit is funded by volatile capital," said Sanjay Mathur, regional economist with UBS Warburg in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 The current account, which covers trade and services, is estimated to have ended the 2005/06 fiscal year with a deficit equivalent to 3 percent of gross domestic product, a sharp expansion from a year-earlier 0.9 percent.  \par \par \uc2 India's benchmark share index <.BSESN> has fallen roughly 20 percent from a record high of 12,671.11 on May 11 as foreign funds cut their investments.  \par \par \uc2 Net foreign investment in stocks since the start of the year has dropped to about $2.4 billion from a peak of nearly $5 billion in early May.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-INDIA-RUPEE|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SNS|SNI|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060601e2610019h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
