\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Rupee thumped again, others mixed  \b0\par\par\uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2006\par 04:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 31 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee dropped to a three-year low and the Indonesian rupiah and Philippine peso fell on Wednesday as regional stock weakness renewed concerns about global risk aversion and a deeper sell-off in emerging markets.  \par \par \uc2  In contrast, the Thai baht <THB=> and Singapore dollar <SGD=> edged higher, drawing support from a rally in the yen and news that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has reaffirmed its commitment to deepen \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reform to make the yuan more flexible.  \par \par \uc2  Markets in Hong Kong and Taiwan were closed for a holiday, while South Korea was shut for local elections.  \par \par \uc2  The Indian rupee <INR=IN> fell to a three-year low around 46.55 per dollar, but trimmed those losses after suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and data that showed \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy grew at a stronger-than-expected annual rate of 9.3 percent in the first quarter.  \par \par \uc2  "The focus of weakness in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is definitely on the rupee," said Callum Henderson, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Standard Chartered.  \par \par \uc2  "India has a large current account deficit and although the GDP numbers were better than expected, this will exacerbate the current account deficit."  \par \par \uc2  And with some Asian equity markets sharply weaker on Wednesday, dealers said regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were not yet immune to the global risk aversion that has dealt a blow to emerging markets this month.  \par \par \uc2  A sharp sell-off on Wall Street on Tuesday helped push India stocks <.BSESN> down more than five percent. Equity markets in Indonesia <.JKSE> and Singapore <.STI> shed more than two percent each.  \par \par \uc2  The rupiah <IDR=> eased about 0.9 percent from late Asia levels on Tuesday to 9,252 per dollar, while the peso <PHP=> weakened to 52.91 per dollar -- about 0.6 percent weaker than Tuesday's 1-1/2-week high around 52.58.  \par \par \uc2  "Many players yesterday were trying to push dollar/peso through the 52.60 area and as that has failed to hold, we have seen short covering," said a trader in Manila.  \par \par \uc2  Other emerging markets were also weak, with the Turkish lira <TRL=> at its lowest since April 2003 and the South African rand <ZAR=> sliding to a near 6-\u189\'00\'BD   month low versus the dollar.  \par \par \uc2  However, some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were holding up well.  \par \par \uc2  The baht <THB=> firmed to a 1-\u189\'00\'BD   week peak at about 38 per dollar, finding support from China \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments on yuan flexibility raised expectations for further gains in the yuan <CNY=CFXS> and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2  "The news about the yuan and more flexibility are not anything new but they are helping the baht," said a Bangkok-based dealer. "We saw dollar/yen sell off and dollar/baht followed."  \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day 0815 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       111.89         111.77       -0.11\par  Sing dlr        1.5757         1.5764       +0.04\par  Baht             38.07          38.16       +0.24\par  Peso             52.78          52.59       -0.36\par  Rupiah         9244.00        9170.00       -0.80\par  Rup