\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee weakens on dollar rally, oil demand  \b0\par\par\uc2 369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2006\par 05:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, May 4 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee ended a little weaker on Thursday as the dollar rose against the yen and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but losses were muted as a booming stock market continued to lend support.  \par \par \uc2 The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased in morning trade on dollar buying by oil companies, defence purchases and suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  imports two-thirds of its oil.  \par \par \uc2 "There was dollar buying by oil companies, and state-run banks perhaps on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a trader at a government-run bank.  \par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN>, which analysts say is overvalued by 5.5 percent on a trade-weighted basis, closed at 44.8950/9050, down from Wednesday's 44.86/87. The rupee had risen against the dollar in the previous three sessions and had touched a one-week high of 44.83 on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose against the yen and other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, while the euro was little moved after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept interest rates on hold at 2.5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was boosted after Japan's finance minister said the G7 did not point to a dollar decline after a meeting of wealthy nations last month.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the rupee was likely to be supported in the near term by persistent inflows into the stock market, which has risen 31 percent since the start of January. Foreign funds have bought Indian equities worth more than $4 billion this year.  \par \par \uc2 Indian shares rose to a new all-time high for a fourth straight session, spurred by robust foreign fund flows and hopes for strong economic growth. But trading was choppy on stretched valuations.  \par \par \uc2 Most of the inflows have been absorbed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which usually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks. Data from \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  released last week showed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose to a record $157.26 billion on April 21 from $155.196 billion a week earlier.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060507e25400018\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
