\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee eases on possible defence payments  \b0\par\par\uc2 280 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2006\par 05:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to open)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, May 4 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee weakened on Thursday, after rising for three straight sessions, possibly due to demand for dollars to make defence payments.  \par \par \uc2 But the rupee may not fall much on expectations for fresh inflows into India's booming stock market, which rose to a new high for the fourth session in a row.  \par \par \uc2 At 10 a.m. (0430 GMT), the partially convertible rupee <INR=IN>, which analysts say is overvalued by 5.5 percent on a trade-weighted basis, was at 44.950/960, down from Wednesday's 44.86/87.  \par \par \uc2 "I think some nationalised banks were buying for making some defence payments," said a dealer with a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was stuck in recent ranges in early Asian trading, having failed to gain traction from strong U.S. data. The euro <EUR=> was little changed at $1.2630.  \par \par \uc2 India's stock market has risen 31 percent since the start of January helped mainly by foreign funds who have bought Indian equities worth more than $4 billion this year.  \par \par \uc2 "There are huge inflows hitting the market all these days," said a dealer with an Indian state-run bank.  \par \par \uc2 Most of the inflows have been absorbed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which usually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks. Data from \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  released last week showed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose to a record $157.26 billion on April 21 from $155.196 billion a week earlier.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ------------------------------------------