\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: US, China, Japan Differ On Imbalances Fix  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Stanley White and Rick Carew  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 993 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2006\par 11:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HYDERABAD, India (Dow Jones)--Finance officials from the U.S., China and Japan agreed Thursday that global imbalances must be fixed, but they continued to disagree on the role that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should play in that adjustment.  \par \par \uc2 Their discussion during a seminar on the sidelines of the Asian Development Bank's annual meeting is the latest in a series of recent public comments that show policy makers around the globe are increasingly worried about the threat to the global economic outlook from these uneven trade and capital flows.  \par \par \uc2 "We are all on the same boat, no one will escape if this boat sinks," said Li Yong, China's vice finance minister. "Each and every nation should shoulder its own responsibility."  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Undersecretary Timothy Adams said during the seminar that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is cautious and needs to move faster.  \par \par \uc2 But China said that a fall of 25% in the U.S. dollar against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could hurt China's structural adjustment and reform program, while Japan Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said a weaker dollar shouldn't be the focus of efforts to resolve the global imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 "Over emphasizing a realignment of exchange rates alone as a means to adjust imbalances could invite market speculation and deal a blow to the global financial market, thus adversely affecting the world economy," Tanigaki told the panel discussion, according to prepared text issued by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki's comments show that despite the Group of Seven leading nations' call last month for emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate versus the dollar, Japan remains wary that such a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy may overreach its intended aims.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese finance minister said late Wednesday that the G7 call for greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility wasn't a call for dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury's Adams said that impediments and distortions in financial markets need to be reduced to help adjust global economic imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. policymakers have repeatedly urged Beijing to allow the yuan to strengthen in order to reduce China's large trade surplus with the U.S. by making Chinese goods more expensive.  \par \par \uc2 But China's vice finance minister defended China's decision to gradually allow the yuan's value to rise and said government plans to increase domestic demand would help correct global imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 "The Chinese government has already started to apply a mix of policy measures including expanding domestic demand,...a floating exchange rate regime, and increasing exports so as to improve the balance of payments situation," said Li.  \par \par \uc2 China's trade surplus tripled in 2005, reaching a record $102 billion.  \par \par \uc2 "China has actually now joined the international community in maintaining global stability and growth," said Li.  \par \par \uc2 Economists have been pondering global imbalances for some time because when a country has large current account, trade and budget surpluses, it relies on excess savings from other parts of the world to finance its growth. On the other hand, countries with large surpluses are diverting their savings to other markets, in some cases depriving their own economies of much-needed investment.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    India Doesn't Plan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Policy Change \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Adams said it is neither likely nor desirable for global imbalances to be unwound quickly and was optimistic that imbalances can be addressed smoothly.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Adams also conceded that the U.S. "needs to do more" by reducing its fiscal budget deficit.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Treasury official called on the International Monetary Fund for its assistance as well.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "One way the IMF can help is to help our understanding of questions like why emerging Asian economies are exporting capital to the U.S.," he said. "No matter what we say, it's viewed through the prism of self interest, so the IMF can remove this in a way we can't."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tanigaki said he couldn't deny that greater flexibility for Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could play a role in reducing massive current account surpluses and deficits in many countries.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But he said that to ensure that policy-makers and market forces adjust global imbalances smoothly, macroeconomic reforms should be the "main driver" of this process.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China should transform itself into an economy led by consumer spending from one driven by "overinvestment, overproduction and brisk exports," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other emerging Asian economies should also stimulate domestic demand, while oil producers should invest more in increasing capacity and diversifying their industrial base, Tanigaki said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. should continue to trim its fiscal budget deficits and European countries should pursue more structural reforms, he continued.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's vice minister of finance also said that a "relatively smooth" change in the U.S. dollar would help countries with their adjustment programs.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Referring to China's exchange rate reform, Li said: "We try our best to (reform the exchange rate) as fast as we can."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said China's shift to a managed float of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the yuan, "reflects China's willingness for further reforms of the exchange rate regime and cooperation with the international community."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's finance minister, P. Chidambaram, signaled that India wouldn't likely alter its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy despite the G7's recent call.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The exchange rate has been quite flexible in recent years, and this has been useful for us...I don't see any immediate change in the exchange rate policy," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India doesn't set a target level for its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  often \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to smooth excess volatility.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also wouldn't likely significantly alter its economic policy even if the unwinding of global imbalances hurts the world economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In case the world economy slows down, India is likely to be less vulnerable as the India growth story is driven by domestic demand," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Stanley White and Rick Carew, Dow Jones Newswires; stanley.white@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Anil Varma contributed to this story.) [ 04-05-06 0909GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020060504e2540002a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
