\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee rises on weak dlr, equity inflows  \b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Mai 2006\par 04:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to open)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, May 3 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee extended gains into a second straight session on Wednesday, bolstered by expectations of foreign inflows into a booming stock market and the dollar's woes against the euro and the yen.  \par \par \uc2 But traders expect dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap gains. High oil prices at just below $75 a barrel could also restrain the rupee, traders said. India imports two-thirds of its oil and firm prices could widen the country's trade deficit.  \par \par \uc2 At 9:25 a.m. (0355 GMT), the partially convertible rupee <INR=IN>, which analysts say is overvalued by 5.5 percent on a trade-weighted basis, was quoted at 44.85/86 against the dollar, up from Tuesday's 44.88/89 when it rose 0.17 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar is floundering a bit overseas and that is pushing up the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the rupee," said the chief dealer at an Indian bank. "Also the local stock market is looking up and we are expecting good flows. But oil is a risk."  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar inched down, hovering near recent lows against the euro and the yen, having failed to extend gains on renewed speculation about further U.S. interest rate rises.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was also boosted after euro zone manufacturing growth hit its highest since September 2000, raising expectations for upbeat language from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  even though borrowing costs should stay on hold this week.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was slightly firmer at $1.2631 <EUR=>, after climbing as high as $1.2667 in New York. The dollar was a tad softer against the Japanese unit at 113.17 yen <JPY=>, not far from seven-month lows around 112.35.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the rupee would draw support from foreign fund inflows into the stock market. Foreign funds have bought Indian equities worth more than $4 billion this year, boosting the stock index <.BSESN> by 30 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Most of the inflows have been absorbed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which usually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks. Data from \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  released on Friday showed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose to a record $157.26 billion on April 21 from $155.196 billion a week earlier.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060506e2530001e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
