\par\par \b \uc2 Indian  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves hit new record on dollar buying  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Anirban Nag  \par \uc2 609 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 April 2006\par 16:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, April 28 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves jumped by more than $2 billion in the week to April 21, an increase which economists said reflected further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of dollars to stem the rise in the rupee.  \par \par \uc2 Data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  released on Friday showed the reserves rose to a record $157.262 billion from the previous record of $155.196 billion in the previous week, enough to cover nearly 14 months of imports.  \par \par \uc2 India is the fifth largest holder of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in Asia after China, Japan, Taiwan and South Korea.  \par \par \uc2 "It's clear that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been very actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said Shuchita Mehta, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Standard Chartered Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "Also given that the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) has been diversifying the reserves holdings, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness against the yen is also reflected in the jump."  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets expressed in U.S. dollar terms included the effect of appreciation or depreciation of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held in its reserves such as the euro, pound sterling and yen.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar dropped to three-week lows against the yen last Friday after a draft communique from the Group of Seven nations indicated they were considering singling out China in their call for more flexibility in exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 Fears of diversification also undermined the dollar that week after Russia's finance minister questioned the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's preeminence as the global reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 It has since fallen further against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , hitting a seven-month low versus the euro.  \par \par \uc2 MORE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?  \par \par \uc2 The RBI's dollar-buying spree, which possibly began as early as February, saw reserves rise by $8 billion in March. It added another $6 billion in the first three weeks in April and traders expect its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say its motive for buying dollars and selling rupees, which it does through state-run banks, has been to keep the rupee down and exports competitive.  \par \par \uc2 But they also say the rupee selling has ensured there are sufficient funds in the system to help the government's 1.53 trillion rupee ($34 billion) borrowing programme go through smoothly.  \par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN>, which analysts say is overvalued by a little more than five percent on a trade-weighted basis, ended Friday at 44.9625/9700, 0.13 percent higher than Thursday's close.  \par \par \uc2 It has gained 0.4 percent this week and dealers say that but for sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling, it could have gained more as the stock market is trading near record highs.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said they expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step up its action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the near term after it announced it was resuming sales of treasury bills under its market stabilisation scheme (MSS).  \par \par \uc2 The scheme is aimed at helping the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  manage excess funds generated by its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and the latest move will drain 25 billion rupees more from the banking system every week.  \par \par \uc2 "It is obvious the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expecting good foreign inflows and they are likely to be present in the market," a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer in a U.S. bank said.  \par \par \uc2 The RBI had suspended sales under the MSS after overnight interbank funding costs rose to a 3-1/2 year high of 8 percent in January due to a redemption of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits and sustained credit growth.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-INDIA-RESERVES|LANGEN|SNI|ABN|M|D|ABX|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060428e24s002uo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
