\par\par \b \uc2 India rupee eases on high oil prices, dlr demand  \b0\par\par\uc2 229 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2006\par 04:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, April 26 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee eased on Wednesday as high crude oil prices fuelled dollar demand from oil companies in a volatile market.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee closed at 45.11 per dollar <INR=IN>, after touching 44.98 in intra-day trade. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at 44.99/45.00 on Tuesday. It hit a near-four-month low of 45.40 earlier in April.  \par \par \uc2 "There was underlying demand. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  presence was not obvious but they were there. There is pressure on the rupee due to high oil prices," said a dealer at an Indian private bank.  \par \par \uc2 Oil stayed above $73 a barrel on Wednesday on falling petrol stocks in the United States, the world's largest consumer.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  via state-run banks in the past few weeks to curb rupee volatility and keep exports competitive.  \par \par \uc2 Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose to a record $155.2 billion on April 14 as a result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  imports about two-thirds of its oil and high prices can push up its trade deficit and put downward pressure on the rupee.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-I