\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee down as state banks keep pressure on  \b0\par\par\uc2 379 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2006\par 04:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, April 21 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee slipped towards the bottom of the week's trading range on Friday, as state-run banks kept it under pressure, and it ended the week close to a recent four-month low.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said state-run banks, likely to be operating on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bought dollars and sold rupees at about 45.10 per dollar <INR=IN> to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a weaker close of 45.1450/1500. It ended Thursday at 45.1050/1150.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's overnight gains against the euro and concern about rising oil prices undermined the rupee early. Record oil prices are putting upward pressure on India's import bill and widening its trade deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said there had been good two-way business with some foreign inflows that looked to be corporate rather than investor inflows, which gave it a boost before state-run banks stepped in to cap its gains.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee hit its lowest in nearly four months last week at 45.40 but according to a JP Morgan trade-weighted index <JPMREER> it remains about 6 percent overvalued.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to absorb foreign fund flows into \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's booming stock market and keep rupee gains in check. Foreign reserves data released on Friday showed reserves rose to a new record of $155.2 billion on April 14.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the rupee would likely see more state-run bank action next week as long as foreign investors continued to put money into Indian stocks.  \par \par \uc2 Foreigners were net sellers of shares earlier in the week but were net buyers on Thursday, when the market rose to another record. The main index <.BSESN> ended flat on Friday after notching up another peak.  \par \par \uc2 "We're looking at the equity market," said one trader at private sector bank. "The trend still seems to be upwards."  \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have pumped about $3.5 billion into Indian shares in 2006, going some way to offseting downward pressure on the rupee from the widening trade deficit.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060424e24l0004s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
