\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee off 4-mth lows, eyes shares and oil  \b0\par\par\uc2 432 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2006\par 05:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to open)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, April 17 (Reuters) - The rupee pulled back from four-month lows on Monday, drawing strength from firmer Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but traders were wary of foreign portfolio investors who had pulled out profits last week.  \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won and the Thai baht jumped in early trade as most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  joined the Japanese yen's rally against the dollar after a newspaper article suggested U.S. rates may not rise much further.  \par \par \uc2 At 9:20 a.m. (0350 GMT) the rupee <INR=IN>, which analysts say is overvalued by 6 percent on a trade-weighted basis, was quoted at 45.0550/0650 a dollar, up from Thursday's close of 45.1650/1800. Banks in the financial hub of Mumbai were closed on Friday for a national holiday.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee had dropped 0.8 percent on Wednesday to 45.18/20 on dollar purchases by foreign funds, which dealers said were probably repatriating some of their stock market profits, and on bids by large oil companies.  \par \par \uc2 State-run banks probably sold dollars on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, helping the rupee to recover from 45.40 -- its weakest point in 2006. \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks to contain excessive volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 "The flows from the foreign funds hold the key," said the chief dealer at an Indian bank. "The dollar's dip against the yen and probably bunched inflows during the weekend are pushing up the rupee right now, but oil is a risk and of course we have to see how the stock market behaves."  \par \par \uc2 India's benchmark stock index fell 3 percent last week, and data showed foreign funds had sold shares worth $356 million in three trading days to Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The stock market is still up 20 percent in 2006, helped by foreign portfolio investment of about $3.7 billion. The inflow has cushioned the rupee from rising oil prices and a widening trade deficit, which some analysts estimate stood at a record $50 billion at the end of financial year on March 31.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. oil futures hit $70 a barrel for the first time in seven and a half months on Monday as tension mounted between Iran and the West over Tehran's nuclear ambitions. India imports about two-thirds of its oil.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060420e24h00059\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
