\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee off 4-mo low as state banks sell dlrs  \b0\par\par\uc2 255 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2006\par 05:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI, April 13 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee pulled back from its lowest in nearly four months to end marginally firmer on Thursday as state-run banks sold dollars probably on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks to contain excessive volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2  The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> ended at 45.1650/1800 a dollar, off 45.40 -- its weakest point in 2006.  \par \par \uc2  Banks in the financial hub of Mumbai are closed on Friday for a national holiday.  \par \par \uc2  The rupee had dropped 0.8 percent on Wednesday to 45.18/20 on dollar purchases by foreign funds, which dealers said were probably repatriating some of their stock market profits, and steady bids by large oil companies.  \par \par \uc2  Shares on the benchmark BSE index <.BSESN> fell 1 percent on Thursday, closing the truncated week down 3 percent as foreign portfolio investors booked profits.  \par \par \uc2  Data showed foreign funds had sold stocks worth $356 million in the last three trading sessions to Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2  The stock index is still up nearly 20 percent since the start of January on foreign investments of $4 billion this year. The inflow has supported the rupee, which is overvalued by 6.5 percent on a trade-weighted basis.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par                   MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index    