\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee off 3-mo low, cbank stays away  \b0\par\par\uc2 311 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2006\par 05:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (updates to mid-morning)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, April 7 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee recovered from a three-month low on Friday, drawing support from a booming stock market and as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed away from absorbing robust foreign equity inflows.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said a statement from Commerce Minister Kamal Nath that Indian exports had crossed $100 billion in 2005/06 also bolstered sentiment.  \par \par \uc2 But volumes in the $16-billion-a-day market were on the low side as traders from the State Bank of India (SBI) stayed away from work demanding an increase in their pension.  \par \par \uc2 Government-run SBI, which has more than 200,000 staff at some 9,000 branches across the country, is the biggest player in India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets. The nationwide strike began on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 At 11:30 a.m. (0600 GMT) the rupee <INR=IN>, which analysts say is overvalued by 7.6 percent on a trade-weighted basis, was quoted at 44.6275/6375 per dollar, up 0.24 percent from a three-month low of 44.7425 hit earlier in the day.  \par \par \uc2 It ended Wednesday at 44.7050/71. The market was closed on Thursday for a local holiday.  \par \par \uc2 "The state-run banks are not there mopping up dollars," said a trader at a government-run bank. "Foreign funds, exporters are all selling (dollars) and taking advantage of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's absence."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks and has been absorbing foreign inflows into the rising stock market and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans raised by corporates in recent sessions. The main share index <.BSESN> has risen 26 percent in 2006, helped by an influx of about $4 billion in foreign funds.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060410e2470004t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
