\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee matches 3-month low as euro dips  \b0\par\par\uc2 601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2006\par 05:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI, April 7 (Reuters) - The rupee matched a recent three-month low on Friday, as the dollar gained on the euro and traders were wary the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under downward pressure by absorbing foreign equity flows.  \par \par \uc2  Volumes in the $16-billion-a-day market were on the low side as traders from the State Bank of India (SBI) stayed away from work demanding an increase in their pension.  \par \par \uc2  Government-run SBI, which has more than 200,000 staff at some 9,000 branches across the country, is the biggest player in India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets. The nationwide strike began on Monday.  \par \par \uc2  At 10:20 a.m. (0450 GMT) the rupee <INR=IN>, which analysts say is overvalued by 7.6 percent on a trade-weighted basis, was quoted at 44.7100/7200 per the dollar, weaker than Wednesday's 44.7050/71 finish. In early trade it matched 44.7425 hit last week, its lowest since Jan. 6.  \par \par \uc2  The Mumbai market was closed on Thursday for a local holiday.  \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's gains against the euro are leading to some demand here," said the chief trader at an Indian bank. "There could be some short-covering going on, but we also expect state-run banks to keep the rupee on a leash."  \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose against the euro on Friday, recovering further from the previous day's seven-month low marked before the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  smothered market expectations for an interest rate rise in May.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks in recent sessions and analysts say the move is to keep the rupee competitive and also to ensure sufficient rupee funds are available when the government starts this year's borrowing programme next week.  \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been mopping up foreign inflows into the rising stock market and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans raised by corporates in recent sessions. The main share index <.BSESN> has risen 26 percent in 2006, helped by an influx of about $4 billion in foreign funds.  \par \par \uc2  RECORD RESERVES, CONVERTIBILITY  \par \par \uc2  Strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  saw reserves jump by more than $5 billion in the first three weeks of March and traders say the total could touch $150 billion when data for the week to March 31 is released later in the day.  \par \par \uc2  India's record \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, a comfortable balance of payments situation and strong economic growth prompted the prime minister last month to ask the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prepare a road map towards fuller rupee convertibility.  \par \par \uc2  But Finance Minister Palaniappan Chidambaram said in a newspaper interview published on Friday said he did not expect the rupee to become fully convertible before 2009.  \par \par \uc2  A six-person \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  committee will begin looking into the proposal from next month and will produce a road map by July 31 this year.  \par \par \uc2  "Ironically the comment may be a modest boost for the rupee as some players had speculated that an accelerated move to full convertibility would actually be a negative for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the Reserve Bank of India added to its $150 billion reserves as a safety cushion ahead of the move," ABN AMRO said in a commentary.  \par \par \uc2  "But with dollar higher versus the yen and a buy-on-dips mentality still in play for dollar/rupee, it is unlikely that rupee will see any immediate benefit."   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -------------------------------------------------------------\par              MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060410e2470004s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
