\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee near 3-mo lows on cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 394 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2006\par 04:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, April 5 (Reuters) - The rupee closed steady near three-month lows on Wednesday, held by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which traders said absorbed robust foreign equity inflows to ensure the overvalued Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not undermine exports.  \par \par \uc2 Volumes in the $16 billion-a-day market were on the lower side as traders from the State Bank of India (SBI) stayed away from work demanding an increase in their pension.  \par \par \uc2 Government-run SBI, which has more than 200,000 staff at some 9,000 branches across the country, is the biggest player in India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets. The indefinite nationwide strike began on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "There were dollar sellers all around, especially the banks which offer custodial services to funds and only one buyer -- the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said the chief trader at a government-run bank. "They started buying dollars at 44.64 levels and continuously bid the dollar up."  \par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN>, which analysts say is overvalued by 7 percent on a trade-weighted basis, ended at 44.7050/71, unchanged from the previous close. It hit 44.7425 last week, its lowest since Jan. 6.  \par \par \uc2 The Mumbai market is closed on Thursday for a local holiday.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks in recent sessions to keep the rupee competitive and also to ensure sufficient rupee funds are available to meet the government's 1.53 trillion rupee ($34.34 billion) borrowing plan that starts next week.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been mopping up foreign inflows into the rising stock market and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans raised by corporates in recent sessions. The main share index <.BSESN> has risen 25 percent in 2006, helped by an influx of about $4 billion in foreign funds.  \par \par \uc2 Strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  saw reserves jump by more than $5 billion in the first three weeks of March and traders say the total could touch $150 billion when data for the week to March 31 is released on April 7. India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves were a record $148.662 billion in the week ended March 24.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -------------------------------------------------------------\par              MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060408e2450001e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
