\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee inches up as dlr trips, cbank eyed  \b0\par\par\uc2 443 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2006\par 05:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to open)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, April 4 (Reuters) - The rupee crept higher on Tuesday, drawing strength from a weaker dollar and a booming stock market, but traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep gains muted.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar slipped versus the euro and the yen after a report cited a Chinese vice parliamentary chief as saying China should stop buying dollar-denominated bonds and should widen the yuan's floating band at an appropriate time.  \par \par \uc2 But volumes in the nearly $16-billion-a-day Indian market are likely to be hit as traders from the country's largest commercial bank, the State Bank of India (SBI), stay away from work demanding an increase in their pension.  \par \par \uc2 Government-run SBI, which has more than 200,000 staff at some 9,000 branches across the country, is the biggest player in the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds markets. The indefinite nationwide strike began on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 At 9:30 a.m. (0400 GMT), the partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> was quoted at 44.5625/5725 against the dollar, 0.07 percent firmer than the previous close of 44.5950/6050. It had hit a two-and-a-half-month low of 44.7425 a week ago.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee, which analysts say is overvalued by 8 percent on a trade-weighted basis, lost 1.95 percent in the financial year that ended last Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar's fall in Asian trade is pushing up the rupee early, but we are also seeing some steady demand for dollars from importers," said a trader at a foreign bank. "Volumes are on the lower side since State Bank is not there, but we expect other government-run banks to buy dollars and cap the rupee's gains."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks to keep the rupee competitive and also to ensure sufficient rupee funds were available to meet the government's 1.53 trillion rupee ($34.34 billion) borrowing plan that starts this week.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has absorbed most of the foreign funds investments into the stock market, whose main index <.BSESN> has risen nearly 23 percent in 2006, helped by an influx of about $3.9 billion in foreign funds.  \par \par \uc2 The funds had pumped in a record $10.7 billion in 2005, helping cushion the rupee from a widening trade deficit.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -------------------------------------------------------------\par              MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060407e2440008q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
