\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee weakens on sustained cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 436 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2006\par 05:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI, April 4 (Reuters) - The rupee snapped a two-session rising streak to end weaker against the dollar on Tuesday, driven down by aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which dealers said led to a short squeeze among some players in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2  The fall came even as the dollar dropped against the euro on expectations of a rate increase in the euro zone area.  \par \par \uc2  The dollar also eased against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after after a report cited a Chinese vice parliamentary chief as saying China should stop buying dollar-denominated bonds and should widen the yuan's floating band at an appropriate time.  \par \par \uc2  "There were some players, especially those with some presence in the offshore forward market, who had sold the dollar, expecting it to fall further in line with the trend overseas," said a trader at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2  "But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seemed to have other ideas and it started buying dollars at 44.60 levels, driving it up and forcing these players to cover shorts."  \par \par \uc2  The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> ended at 44.7050/7150 per dollar, 0.2 percent weaker than the previous close of 44.5950/6050 and very close to a 2-\u189\'00\'BD   month low of 44.7425 a week ago.  \par \par \uc2  Volumes in the nearly $16-billion-a-day Indian market were hit as traders from the country's largest commercial bank, the State Bank of India (SBI), stayed away from work demanding an increase in their pension.  \par \par \uc2  Government-run SBI, which has more than 200,000 staff at some 9,000 branches across the country, is the biggest player in the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets. The indefinite nationwide strike began on Monday.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks to keep the rupee competitive and also to ensure sufficient rupee funds are available to meet the government's 1.53 trillion rupee ($34.34 billion) borrowing plan that starts this week.  \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has absorbed most of the foreign fund investments into the stock market, whose main index <.BSESN> has risen nearly 23 percent in 2006 helped by an influx of about $3.9 billion in foreign funds.  \par \par \uc2  The funds pumped in a record $10.7 billion in 2005, helping cushion the rupee from a widening trade deficit.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -------------------------------------------------------------\par              MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060407e2440008p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
