\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee edges up, but cbank checks gains  \b0\par\par\uc2 553 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2006\par 04:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, April 3 (Reuters) - The rupee inched up on Monday, supported by broadly firmer Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and strong foreign inflows into a booming stock market, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  checked sharp gains, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Volumes in the nearly $16 billion-a-day market took a slight beating, brokers said, as traders from the country's largest commercial bank, the State Bank of India (SBI), stayed away from work demanding an increase in their pension.  \par \par \uc2 The government-run SBI has more 200,000 staff at some 9,000 branches across the country, and is one of the biggest players in the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds markets. Monday was the first day of an indefinite nation-wide strike.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee <INR=IN> ended at 44.5950/6050 a dollar, marginally firmer than the previous close of 44.6125/6225, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  capping gains at 44.59 against the dollar. It hit a two-and-a-half-month low of 44.7425 on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said that sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped keep the rupee competitive, but also ensured sufficient liquidity to meet the government's 1.53 trillion rupee ($34.34 billion) borrowing plan that starts this week.  \par \par \uc2 "The strategy is very clear," said a trader at a foreign bank. "The foreign inflows will be absorbed no matter what and only if the dollar weakens or Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise could we see them letting the rupee rise, (and then) only marginally."  \par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose to a record $148.662 billion in the week ended March 24. Strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has seen reserves jump by more than $5 billion in the first three weeks of March and traders say the total could touch $150 billion when data for the week to March 31 is released on April 7.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee, which analysts say is overvalued by nearly 8 percent on a trade-weighted basis, lost 1.95 percent in the financial year that ended last Friday.  \par \par \uc2 But the rupee rose 0.18 percent on Friday on the back of data which showed India's current account deficit in the October-December quarter narrowing to $3.85 billion, from a $5.06 billion shortfall in the July-September quarter.  \par \par \uc2 "Indeed in the subsequent quarter, Reserve Bank of India's reserves rose $11.5 billion to $148.7 billion on the back of aggressive dollar purchases by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up rupee liquidity as capital inflows remain buoyant and export growth stayed healthy," Standard Chartered said in a daily commentary.  \par \par \uc2 "Further improvement in current account balances should help limit the risk of a sharp depreciation in the rupee. This is especially the case as we expect capital inflows to remain strong."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The main share index <.BSESN> has risen 22 percent in 2006, helped by an influx of about $3.9 billion in foreign funds. They had pumped in a record $10.7 billion, helping cushion the rupee from a widening trade deficit.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -------------------------------------------------------------\par              MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index      