\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee rises on euro, stocks; wary of cbank  \b0\par\par\uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 04:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to open)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, March 31 (Reuters) - The rupee pulled further away on Friday from near two-and-a-half-month lows in the previous session, helped by a rise in the euro and supported by robust foreign flows into the booming stock market.  \par \par \uc2 But traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has mopped up most of the foreign investments during the past month, was likely to keep gains muted.  \par \par \uc2 At 9:20 a.m. (0350 GMT), the rupee <INR=IN> was quoted at 44.59/61 against the dollar, 0.2 percent higher than Wednesday's close of 44.6950/7050. It had hit a two-and-a-half-month low of 44.7425 on Tuesday, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 Banks in the financial hub of Mumbai were closed on Thursday for a local holiday.  \par \par \uc2 India's key stock index ended at a new closing high of 11,307 points on Thursday, up 1.1 percent. It has risen nearly 20 percent in 2006, helped by foreign inflow of more than $3.7 billion.  \par \par \uc2 "Yesterday's stock market rally and the dollar's fall against some of the majors has lifted the rupee," said the chief trader at an Indian bank. "But my guess is that they (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) are likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon and cap the gains."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb volatility and keep exports competitive.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee has lost 0.8 percent from a high of 44.25 at the start of March, as analysts estimate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has mopped up nearly $4-5 billion in the past four weeks.  \par \par \uc2 Most of the inflows have been from foreign funds who have pumped more than $3.5 billion into stocks since the start of the year, after putting in a record $10.7 billion in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said they would also eye October-December quarter current account deficit data, to be released later in the day. A widening deficit could weigh on the rupee in the medium term, although foreign inflows are expected to provide support in the short-run.  \par \par \uc2 The current account registered a $5.4 billion deficit for the whole of 2004/05 (April-March), the first shortfall in four years and was at a $7.66 billion deficit in the July-September quarter.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -------------------------------------------------------------\par              MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060403e23v0005a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
