\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee capped by cbank, down 1.95 pct in FY06  \b0\par\par\uc2 509 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 04:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI, March 31 (Reuters) - The rupee moved away on Friday from near two-and-a-half-month lows struck in the previous session on strong foreign investment in the booming stock market, but traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  absorbed most of the inflows.  \par \par \uc2  Steady \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has seen \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves jump to a record $148.66 billion by March 24.  \par \par \uc2  Despite Friday's rise, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which analysts say is overvalued by nearly 8 percent on a trade-weighted basis, has lost 1.95 percent in the financial year to March 2006.  \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue its recent policy of pumping rupees into the system, via \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as a way of ensuring there is sufficient liquidity and ensuring a smooth passage for the government's borrowing plan in the new financial year to March 2007.  \par \par \uc2  The rupee has lost 0.8 percent from a high of 44.25 at the start of March, and analysts estimate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has mopped up nearly $5-6 billion in the past four weeks.  \par \par \uc2  The rupee <INR=IN> ended at 44.6125/6225 against the dollar, 0.18 percent higher than Wednesday's close of 44.6950/7050. It had hit a two-and-a-half-month low of 44.7425 on Tuesday, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2  Banks in the financial hub of Mumbai were closed on Thursday for a local holiday.  \par \par \uc2  "There were plenty of inflows, but it all got mopped up by the state-run banks acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a senior trader at a foreign bank said.  \par \par \uc2  "By \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowing foreign investors to come in at a cheaper rate. It is benefitting the foreign investors the most."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb volatility and keep exports competitive.  \par \par \uc2  India's main stock index ended  slightly lower on Friday as investors locked in profits from the fourth successive quarter of gains, with a 20 percent increase in the index since the start of January.  \par \par \uc2  The index has been boosted in 2006 by foreign inflows of more than $3.7 billion.  \par \par \uc2  Traders said October-December released quarter current account deficit data had little impact on the rupee, although they expect the deficit for the January-March to widen on higher oil imports.  \par \par \uc2  India's current account deficit in the October-December quarter stood at $3.85 billion, narrower than a revised $5.06 billion deficit in the July-September quarter, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday.  \par \par \uc2  The current account registered a $5.4 billion deficit for the whole of 2004/05 (April-March), the first shortfall in four years, and was at $7.66 billion in the July-September quarter.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -------------------------------------------------------------\par              MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)             