\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee at near 2-\u189\'00\'BD mth lows on dlr, cbank  \b0\par\par\uc2 435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 04:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, March 29 (Reuters) - The rupee dropped to near two-and-a-half month lows on Wednesday, pushed down by a firm dollar and sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  which mopped up strong foreign investment flows, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were also weaker after the U.S. Federal Reserve raised interest rates and left the door open for more increases, which could help boost the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee, which analysts say is overvalued by more than 8 percent on a trade-weighted basis, ended at 44.6950/7050 against the dollar -- near its previous session trough of 44.7425, the lowest since Jan. 6, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 It ended 0.11 percent weaker than Tuesday's 44.6450/6550. Banks in the financial hub of Mumbai are closed on Thursday for a holiday.  \par \par \uc2 "It was the same old story repeated today," said a trader at a foreign bank, refering to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The rupee fell due to the dollar's Fed-inspired rally overnight, but was starting to regain some ground before the state-run banks entered and absorbed most of the inflows at 44.69-44.71 levels."  \par \par \uc2 The Fed raised rates by a quarter of a percentage point to 4.75 percent, as expected, and said in a statement that more policy tightening "may be needed", which many traders saw as a signal for another rate increase in May.  \par \par \uc2 Higher U.S. interest rates would boost the dollar's yield advantage over other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and could weigh on rate-sensitive capital flows into India in the medium term.  \par \par \uc2 But traders said in the short run, robust foreign investment in India's surging stock market and inflows from companies which have raised loans overseas would support the rupee.  \par \par \uc2 India's key index ended at a new closing high of 11,183.48 point, up 0.88 percent. It has risen nearly 18 percent in 2006, helped by a foreign influx of more than $3.5 billion in funds.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb volatility and keep exports competitive.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to inject rupees into the domestic money market and ease some of the tightness the banking system has seen in the past three months.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par              MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|RBN|SNS|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060401e23t0002h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
