\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee off 2-1/2-mnth low, but cbank caps it  \b0\par\par\uc2 465 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 04:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI, March 28 (Reuters) - The rupee rebounded from early lows to end firmer on Tuesday, aided by robust foreign investment in the surging stock market, but traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  curbed sharper gains.  \par \par \uc2  Traders said sentiment was also cautious ahead of a widely expected interest rate rise by the U.S. Federal Reserve later on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2  An interest rate increase narrows the rate differentials between the United States and India, a factor that could weigh on rate-sensitive foreign capital inflows.  \par \par \uc2  The partially convertible rupee <INR=IN> ended at 44.6450/6550 against the dollar, off an early trough of 44.7425 which was the lowest since Jan. 6, according to Reuters data. It ended marginally firmer than Monday's close of 44.6650/6750.  \par \par \uc2  "There were very good inflows from foreign funds, companies which have raised \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans abroad, but there was only one buyer," said a trader at a foreign bank. "The state-run banks were there at 44.65 levels for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and they did not allow the rupee to appreciate."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb volatility and keep exports competitive.  \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has absorbed most of the foreign flows into Indian stocks. The benchmark share index has risen nearly 18 percent in 2006, helped by an influx of more than $3.5 billion.  \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to inject rupees into the domestic money market and ease some of the tightness the banking system has seen in the past three months.  \par \par \uc2  Tight money has forced some banks to raise one-year certificate of deposits at 8.5 to 9.0 percent. The higher borrowing costs come even as banks are under pressure from the government to hold lending rates steady.  \par \par \uc2  Government officials fear a rise in banks' lending rates could derail the robust investment cycle in Asia's third-largest economy.  \par \par \uc2  Bonds and the money market have suffered from a cash crunch in recent months and last week Finance Minister Palaniappan Chidambaram said he hoped the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would act to improve liquidity after meeting Indian bank chiefs this week.  \par \par \uc2  Traders said they would eye the dollar, which maintained a weaker tone as investors awaited the result of the Fed meeting. The Fed is expected to raise the federal funds rate to 4.75 percent, its 15th straight rate rise since mid-2004.  \par \par \uc2  India's key reverse repo rate is at 5.5 percent.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par              MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|RBN|SNS|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060331e23s0004q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
