\par\par \b \uc2 India rupee ends down, cbank seen fielding inflows  \b0\par\par\uc2 355 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 04:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, March 10 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee ended slightly lower on Friday as selling by state-run banks, possibly on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , got the upper hand over foreign investment inflows, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) uses state-run banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to smooth volatility and keep Indian exports competitive, and traders said those banks had been snapping up dollars during the session to absorb inflows into the stock market.  \par \par \uc2 "There's been (dollar) buying by the state-run banks and selling by all of us," said one trader at a smaller government-owned bank.  \par \par \uc2 Another trader at a foreign bank said the nationalised banks had bought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fairly aggressively around 44.48 per dollar <INR=IN>.  \par \par \uc2 "I would think it was for the RBI. It seems that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not allowing the rupee to gain. When it (dollar/rupee) goes below the previous day's close, they seem to be coming in," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee ended at 44.4950/5050 per dollar, weaker than Thursday's close of 44.48/49. It has shed about 0.4 percent since last Friday.  \par \par \uc2 It fell to 44.56 moments after the open, not far off Thursday's two-week low around 44.5850, but dealers said foreign inflows at the start of share trading had given it a lift.  \par \par \uc2 The main share index <.BSESN> rose nearly 2 percent to a record close on Friday, with banks and blue-chip software services surging on renewed fund buying.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have bought more than $3 billion worth of shares this year, offsetting downward pressure on the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a widening current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Overseas, the yen held near a two-week low against the dollar <JPY=> as expectations remained that Japanese interest rates would remain near zero while other central banks raise them.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par              MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|Table|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060313e23a0005k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
