\par\par \b \uc2 India Fin Min: On Path For Sustained 8% Growth  \b0\par\par\uc2 447 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 05:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI (Dow Jones)--India's Finance Minister P. Chidambaram said Friday that the country may soon be able to sustain an annual economic growth rate of 8% on the back of higher savings and investment rates.  \par \par \uc2 "We are on the road that will take us to sustained 8% growth and also give us the capability for 10% growth," Chidambaram said, addressing a Indo-U.S. seminar on economic cooperation.  \par \par \uc2 The Indian economy is expected to grow 8.l% in the current fiscal year. [ 03-03-06 0515GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Chidambaram said his ministry hopes to see the investment rate increase to 36% of gross domestic product from the current 30%, while it is aiming for the savings rate to rise to 30% of GDP from the current 29%.  \par \par \uc2 The minister didn't specify a timeframe for these targets.  \par \par \uc2 "We need investments. The federal budget for the next financial year has given increased attention to infrastructure sectors like power, roads, and the oil sector," Chidambaram said.  \par \par \uc2 He said financial sectors like insurance, pension funds and banking are likely to see more reform by the year 2009.  \par \par \uc2 "We are committed to reforms, but it will be at our own pace," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign companies are pushing the Indian government to allow greater foreign investment in the financial sector, although such moves face strong opposition from the current Congress Party-led government's communist allies.  \par \par \uc2 On insurance, Chidambaram said that 26% cap on foreign direct investment shouldn't pose an obstacle for new companies entering India, but added that the restriction hurts plans of foreign companies already in India and seeking to expand.  \par \par \uc2 "We have to be patient....We are discussing with our allies. I am confident of persuading them," he said, in reference to efforts to pass an amendment to the insurance law, increasing the FDI cap to 49%.  \par \par \uc2 Chidambaram also said India will require some more time before it can open up full capital-account convertibility.  \par \par \uc2 "There are certain parameters (for achieving full capital-account convertibility) like the fiscal deficit, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, inflation and non-performing assets of banks....We have come a long way, but there is still some way to go," he said.  \par \par \uc2 India currently follows partial capital-account convertibility, with the rupee kept under a managed float by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Though the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  say that the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value is determined by the market, the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in case of excessive volatility.  \par \par \uc2 - By P.R. Venkat, Dow Jones Newswires; +91-11-2307 4020; venkat.pr@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Michael Kitchen [ 03-03-06 0602GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060303e2330004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
