\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-Indian rupee slips as foreign investors snap up dlr  \b0\par\par\uc2 454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Februar 2006\par 14:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to additional subscribers)  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, Feb 22 (Reuters) - The rupee fell to its lowest level in almost seven weeks on Wednesday, driven down by strong demand for dollars from foreign investors that dealers said was related to positioning in the offshore forwards markets.  \par \par \uc2 The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <INR=IN> suffered its biggest one-day drop so far this year, with dollar-related buying to fund oil imports adding to rupee weakness, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 It closed at 44.6350/6450 per dollar, down almost 0.5 percent from Tuesday's close. The rupee fell as far as 44.65 per dollar, the weakest level since Jan. 6, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said foreign investors were buying dollars in India and selling them in the offshore non-deliverable forwards (NDF) <PNDF> market, where the dollar was trading at a higher rate than in the onshore forwards market.  \par \par \uc2 They said there was an arbitrage opportunity of about 0.10 rupees between the onshore and offshore rupee markets.  \par \par \uc2 "There is an arbitrage play going that is being cashed in by some players," said the chief dealer at a state bank.  \par \par \uc2 Nationalised banks were seen buying dollars for rupees and traders said this may have been on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or corporates.  \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) was suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , via the state banks, in the market last week to prevent rupee gains from blunting exporter competitiveness.  \par \par \uc2 "The state banks have been in and I don't know if its for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or not," said another trader in Mumbai.  \par \par \uc2 "I wouldn't be surprised if it is not for the RBI. There has generally been a lot of dollar buying, mostly from foreign banks."  \par \par \uc2 Another trader said an expectation that strong foreign capital inflows into Indian stocks may start to slow, especially in the face of India's widening trade deficit, also hurt the rupee.  \par \par \uc2 India's benchmark stock index <.BSESN> touched a fresh record high on Wednesday. Strong buying by foreign investors have been a key driver behind the stock market rally -- foreign fund inflows have topped $1.7 billion so far this year.  \par \par \uc2 "Stock market inflows are the reason why the dollar has not been able to strengthen against the rupee in the same way it has against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Pravesh Malhotra, a trader at Centurion Bank. "But we could see a correction in capital flows from here and that could put upward pressure on dollar/rupee."   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par              MARKET SNAPSHOT\par  Bombay Sensitive Index          <.BSESN>\par  Indian rupee (/$)               <INR=IN>\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE (UPDATE 3, REPEAT)|LANGEN|Table|SNS|SNI|ABN|M|RBN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060225e22m000ad\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
